</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fast-in-Slow Reasoning</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by dual system theories of human cognition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, we propose a <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fast-in-Slow</span> paradigm for Nav-R1, which tightly couples deliberate semantic reasoning with rapid action execution.
As shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Nav-R1: Reasoning and Navigation in Embodied Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, this design addresses the tension between accurate long-horizon planning and low-latency control in dynamic embodied environments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object goal navigation results on HM3D-OVON <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>.</span> <sup id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup> denotes zero-shot methods.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:118.3pt;vertical-align:-57.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-40.4pt,13.9pt) scale(0.810182347572754,0.810182347572754) ;">
